      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To examine interprofessional models of care and care delivery for cancer survivorship, focusing on nursing as key providers of care."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DATA SOURCES",
            "content": "National summary statements and literature review."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The need for cancer survivorship care is established. Treatment summaries and survivorship care plans are mandated documents expected to guide the delivery of survivorship care. However, the optimal delivery method, infrastructure, provider, and (cost-) effectiveness for the delivery of cancer survivorship care is unknown. Utilizing commonly occurring scenarios in cancer survivorship, this article discusses the visit structure, content care delivery structure, and possible care providers."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "IMPLICATIONS FOR NURSING PRACTICE",
            "content": "These real-life situations can help the cancer care community to develop optimal algorithms of care and identify members of the interprofessional team for the survivorship care delivery."
          }
        ]
      },
